    "body": "Seems like this is actually not agreed upon by the three implementations I thought I had checked previously. Firefox directly uses its pipeline's state to determine whether playback has ended, which sticks even after the attribute changes, but Chromium and WebKit do change it when the attribute is removed as the spec says.\n\nI'd argue it doesn't make a lot of sense as written, though. Changing the loop attribute doesn't change the fact that the element _already_ reached the end of the media resource, so it seems to me that the steps to loop the element would not trigger again when `play()` is called. Chromium and WebKit both _do_ loop the element in that scenario.\n\nHowever, the way that Chromium deals with this is by instead treating the `loop` attribute as false when the play steps ask whether playback has ended, causing playback to restart through those steps instead of the looping ones. That solution is also reasonable, and probably the path of least resistance given that two implementations agree on this behavior.",
    "created_at": "2026-09-29T13:12:57Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-09-29T13:14:23Z",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/11792#discussion_r4133855418",
